<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Scoped as narrowly as the job allows, with one exception that has to be argued for.

  canRetrieveWindowContent is required to read the address bar and page text of another
  browser, which is what closes the "you deleted the app but its in-app browser still
  opens the site" side door and what lets Aegis judge a page in Chrome on its content
  rather than its name.

  flagIncludeNotImportantViews is the exception, and it is not optional. Chrome marks
  large parts of rendered web content as "not important for accessibility" — a reasonable
  default for a screen reader, which wants headings and links rather than every wrapper.
  Without this flag those nodes simply do not exist as far as this service is concerned,
  so a walk of a search results page returns the toolbar and almost nothing else. That is
  precisely the bug where Chrome silently failed to block what the built-in browser caught.

  typeViewScrolled is here because a page's content arrives as it is scrolled: the first
  tree after navigation is often nearly empty, and without this the only chances to look
  are the load event and a six-second timer. It is also how an endless-scroll run is
  counted at all.

  typeViewClicked is the only way to refuse a screen *before* it renders. Window content
  cannot be read until a window exists, so screen recognition can act on the first frame
  but never sooner. A tap on the Reels tab arrives earlier than that, and is a clearer
  signal besides: it is an intention, not a description of what happens to be on screen.
  Only the tapped node's view id and label are read, and only in the apps whose surfaces
  the user has selected.

  flagReportViewIds earns its place twice over: it is what makes both the address bar
  lookup and short-form surface recognition possible. Recognising a screen by its layout
  ids rather than by its words means nothing anyone posted is ever read to do it.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeViewScrolled|typeViewClicked"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagReportViewIds|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows|flagIncludeNotImportantViews"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="200" />
